{
  "analysis": "The terminal output keeps getting truncated to the last command chunk, so I cannot see the earlier outputs. I should use a strategy that writes results to files and then uses `tail -n` on specific files, or use `cat` on very small ranges. I need to collect: ELF program headers, syscall numbers/wrappers, and key source behavior. Let me use Python to extract only the relevant grep matches to a file and then view the first few lines.",
  "plan": "Create compact summary files: ELF headers to /tmp/elf_info.txt (already done), grep matches to /tmp/syscalls.txt, then print only the first 80 lines of each. Also use `wc -l` to know file sizes. I will run one command at a time by putting all output in a single small script.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "{ echo '=== ELF ==='; cat /tmp/elf_info.txt; echo '=== SYSCALL GREP ==='; grep -nE \"syscall|__asm__|#define SYS|NR_|register|\\\\$2|\\\\$7|\\\\$v0|\\\\$a3|addiu|li \" /app/doomgeneric/doomgeneric/my_stdlib.c | head -80; echo '=== STDLIB LINES 1-120 ==='; sed -n '1,120p' /tmp/stdlib_lines.txt; } > /tmp/summary1.txt\nwc -l /tmp/summary1.txt /tmp/stdlib_lines.txt /tmp/elf_info.txt\nhead -120 /tmp/summary1.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}
